# Release-tag signer roster (DP-A). The Release workflow verifies every v* tag's SSH signature
# against THIS file as committed on main (never the tag's own tree), so adding or rotating a
# release-signing key is a reviewed main-branch change and a pushed tag can never vouch for
# itself. While this roster is empty the Release workflow fails closed with a pointer here.
#
# OWNER ACTION: add each release-signing SSH PUBLIC key on its own line, allowed_signers format:
#   <email-or-identity> namespaces="git" <keytype> <base64-key> <comment>
# <keytype> is ssh-ed25519 for a software key, or sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com for a hardware-backed
# FIDO/U2F security key; ssh-keygen -Y verify (invoked below via git verify-tag) accepts both key
# types, since verifying a signature is a public-key operation needing only the line below, never
# the signer's hardware.
# Sign tags with: git -c gpg.format=ssh -c user.signingkey=<path-to-private> tag -s vX.Y.Z
# The roster can carry more than one key at once, so rotating to a new signing key keeps tags cut
# with the previous key verifiable; retire a superseded key by deleting its line once nothing
# still relies on it. These keys identify WHO may cut a release tag; this is unrelated to the
# offline channel-signing hybrid key (which never leaves the owner's custody and is what
# customers' engines pin).

